    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok that makes sense. so it's basically just like a middleman holding the cash so the seller doesnt run off with it before the paperwork is done? is there any specific risk if we didn't use one?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Exactly—escrow acts as a secure middleman so neither party can misuse the funds before the deal closes. Skipping escrow would expose you to risks like the seller receiving your money before the title is clear, or you losing your deposit if the seller backs out and there’s no neutral party to enforce the contract. It also helps ensure all prorated taxes, utilities, and liens are paid correctly, which could otherwise lead to costly post‑closing disputes. Using escrow is the standard way to protect both sides in a Canadian real‑estate transaction.",
      "source": "target",
